  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $x=\\pi ,$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )=1-(-1)=2 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/6b8ac504d93007049795ee92eb8865225751314478d68ace67878e27ed471b24.png)",
